---
import OrcaDocs from "../../../../components/OrcaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { ORCA_REPO } from "../../../../data/orca";

const HETZNER = `${ORCA_REPO}/blob/main/docs/hetzner.md`;
const O = `${ORCA_REPO}/issues`;

const description =
  "Deploying Orca on one Hetzner server: the scripts from a release, deploys that replace only what changed, the UI and apps without downtime, overlapping schedulers so a deploy doesn't pause runs, rollbacks, settings from hetzner.env, secrets encrypted with SOPS, rescaling the server, and what comes next.";
---

<OrcaDocs href="/s/orca/docs/deploy" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">Deploying</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    On one Hetzner server, a deploy shouldn't cost a run, and a bad one should be easy to
    undo. The scripts that do it come with each Orca release. This page is the outline;
    <a href={HETZNER} rel="noopener">docs/hetzner.md</a> in the repository is the full
    runbook. On AWS, <a href="/s/orca/docs/cli#aws">orca aws</a> makes and updates the stack.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#scripts">The scripts</a>
    <a href="#deploys">Deploys</a>
    <a href="#overlap">Overlapping schedulers</a>
    <a href="#rollbacks">Rollbacks</a>
    <a href="#settings">Settings and secrets</a>
    <a href="#rescaling">Rescaling</a>
    <a href="#next">Next</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="scripts">The scripts</h2>
  <p>
    Since 0.19.0 the deploy tooling is a release asset, so deploying needs no checkout of
    orca.mojo. It unpacks to <code>orca-hetzner-&lt;version&gt;/</code>, and the scripts
    print that version:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`curl -fsSL https://github.com/magmalake/orca.mojo/releases/latest/download/orca-hetzner.tar.gz | tar -xz
cd orca-hetzner-<version>
cp hetzner.env.example hetzner.env     # fill in every line
./up.sh --dry-run                      # prints every hcloud and ssh command; runs none
./up.sh`} />
  <p>
    <code>up.sh</code> makes the firewall, the Volume and the server, puts the settings
    and secrets on it and starts the containers: Postgres, the scheduler, the UI, the
    sampler, the apps and the Cloudflare tunnel
    (<a href={`${HETZNER}#first-setup`} rel="noopener">First setup</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="deploys">Deploys</h2>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./deploy.sh a1b2c3d                    # the image at that tag; starts again what changed
./deploy.sh a1b2c3d console=latest     # one app on another tag
./deploy.sh --all a1b2c3d              # every container again, changed or not`} />
  <p>
    <strong>Only what changed starts again</strong> (since 0.18.0). <code>orca-stack
    apply</code> compares each container's image, env file, flags and mounts with what it
    runs, and leaves it alone when nothing differs; <code>orca-stack plan</code> says what
    an apply would do. A census-only deploy replaces the console and nothing else, and
    Postgres is never restarted.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The UI and the apps without downtime</strong> (since 0.18.0). A new UI or app
    starts beside the old one, takes its name on the network once it's healthy (the UI's
    <code>/healthz</code>), and the old one finishes its requests and stops. One that
    never gets healthy is removed and the old one keeps serving. Remote workers talk only
    to the UI, so their runs go on through a deploy.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The scheduler.</strong> Restarting it kills the runs it holds, so when the plan
    replaces it, <code>deploy.sh</code> pauses the pipelines, waits for the scheduler's
    running runs (not those on remote workers), backs the database up, applies, registers
    the pipelines from the new image and unpauses what it paused. When the plan doesn't
    replace the scheduler, nothing is paused.
  </p>

  <h2 id="overlap">Overlapping schedulers</h2>
  <p>
    Since 0.18.0, with <code>orca-stack configure OVERLAP=1</code> (off by default), a
    deploy that replaces the scheduler doesn't pause. The new scheduler starts beside the
    old one and takes the lead, and the old one starts nothing more, finishes its runs
    under its own image and exits (<a href={`${O}/232`} rel="noopener">#232</a>). A
    migration that raises the schema's floor refuses the overlap, and the deploy then
    pauses and waits as above, saying why.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rollbacks">Rollbacks</h2>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./deploy.sh --rollback                 # back to the tags deployed before
ssh root@<ip> orca-stack apply --app console=<sha>   # only an app`} />
  <p>
    Each deploy keeps the tags it replaced and, when it replaces anything, a database
    backup first (since 0.16.0). <code>--rollback</code> applies the previous tags and, if
    the scheduler was replaced, registers the pipelines again, so runs start with the code
    that's deployed. Postgres isn't rolled back: an older orca runs on a newer schema
    whose migrations since are additive. Only a migration that raises the floor needs the
    backup restored (<a href={`${HETZNER}#rolling-back`} rel="noopener">Rolling back</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="settings">Settings and secrets</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Settings from <code>hetzner.env</code></strong> (since 0.18.0). Every deploy,
    rollbacks too, brings the server's settings and secret files in line with
    <code>hetzner.env</code> and prints the names of what changed, never a value. So
    <code>hetzner.env</code> is where a setting changes: a hand edit to
    <code>/etc/orca/settings.env</code> is undone by the next deploy, and an extra line
    belongs in <code>EXTRA_ENV_FILE</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Secrets encrypted with SOPS</strong> (since 0.19.0). A secret file that
    <code>hetzner.env</code> names as <code>*.sops</code> or <code>*.sops.&lt;ext&gt;</code>
    is decrypted with <code>sops -d</code> on its way to the server and never written to a
    file on the laptop, so the laptop keeps one age key instead of plain tokens
    (<a href={`${HETZNER}#secrets-on-your-laptop-sops`} rel="noopener">Secrets on your
    laptop</a>). One <code>SECRETS_FILE</code> can hold every secret, decrypted once,
    so a Secure Enclave key asks for Touch ID once per deploy
    (<a href={`${O}/265`} rel="noopener">#265</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="rescaling">Rescaling</h2>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./rescale.sh up                        # CAX21 -> CAX41, keeping the disk
./rescale.sh down                      # CAX41 -> CAX21`} />
  <p>
    For an import, <code>rescale.sh</code> changes the server's type. It pauses every
    pipeline and waits for the running runs, those on remote workers too, since the UI
    they talk to goes down with the server; backs up, changes the type, waits for the
    scheduler and unpauses what it paused. The server is down for a few minutes
    (<a href={`${HETZNER}#rescaling-for-an-import`} rel="noopener">Rescaling for an
    import</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="next">Next</h2>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>
      <a href={`${O}/247`} rel="noopener">orca #247</a>: <code>orca deploy</code> in the CLI,
      with the scripts built into the binary and the settings in the app's repository.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={`${O}/250`} rel="noopener">orca #250</a>: a dev stack and a release flow
      that promotes the image tested there to production.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={`${O}/263`} rel="noopener">orca #263</a>: deploying on GCP's ARM machines
      beside Hetzner, and the scripts working against any host with SSH.
    </li>
  </ul>
</OrcaDocs>
